<onlyinclude>'''High-bandwidth memory''' (HBM) is stacked DRAM with through-silicon-via (TSV) connections and a wide package-level data interface. Its principal design objective is aggregate memory throughput; device peak bandwidth and delivered workload bandwidth must be measured separately.</onlyinclude>

== Standards and organization ==
JEDEC's JESD235A (2016 announcement) and JESD235B (17 December 2018 announcement) are historical revisions, not interchangeable with later HBM generations. JESD235B specifies a 1,024-bit interface organized into eight 128-bit channels, up to 2.4 Gbit/s per pin, and supported 2-, 4-, 8- and 12-high TSV stack configurations with announced capacity up to 24 GB per stack. These are standard capabilities, not a guarantee for every implementation [https://www.cdrinfo.com/D7/content/jedec-updates-high-bandwidth-memory-standard JEDEC 2018 announcement].

AMD's [https://docs.amd.com/r/en-US/pg276-axi-hbm/HBM-Topology PG276 HBM topology] describes one controller implementation with eight channels per stack and two 64-bit pseudo-channels per 128-bit channel. Pseudo-channel behavior, address mapping and scheduling must be verified against the target device/controller documentation; they are not universal properties of all revisions.

== Peak bandwidth derivation ==
For ''W'' physical data pins and effective signaling rate ''R'' bits per second per pin, the ideal data-interface rate (decimal bytes/s) is

:<math>B_{peak}=\frac{WR}{8}.</math>

For the JESD235B announced example, <math>W=1024</math> and <math>R=2.4\times10^9</math> bit/s/pin, yielding <math>B_{peak}=307.2\times10^9</math> bytes/s = '''307.2 GB/s''' per stack. Four independently saturated identical stacks would nominally yield '''1,228.8 GB/s'''. Neither number includes effective utilization or demonstrates a measured workload rate. Capacity and stack count must be evaluated separately.

An explicit utilization model is <math>B_{delivered}=\eta B_{peak}</math>, where <math>0\leq\eta\leq1</math> is a ''measured'', workload-specific aggregate efficiency factor, not a universal constant. Bank conflicts, row-buffer misses, request granularity, command overhead, read/write turnaround, controller arbitration, refresh, thermal constraints, interconnect contention and software locality can reduce <math>\eta</math>. Random-access latency is a distinct metric.

== Architectural evaluation ==
A bandwidth-bound kernel with <math>Q</math> transferred bytes and <math>F</math> operations has arithmetic intensity <math>I=F/Q</math>. A simplified roofline bound is <math>P\leq\min(P_{compute},I B_{delivered})</math>, assuming bytes counted at the measured HBM interface, consistent units and negligible unmodeled bottlenecks. It is an analytical bound, not a performance prediction. Different cache hit rates and reuse strategies change <math>Q</math>; controllers and interconnects may limit throughput before the DRAM interface.

Measure sustained read, write and mixed traffic separately with stated transfer sizes, address patterns, channel distribution, working-set size, temperature, clock state and concurrency. Report achieved bytes/time and tail latency, not only nominal pin rate. Compare with conventional DRAM only at matched capacity, power-measurement boundary, generation and workload. Power efficiency, repairability and cost cannot be inferred from pin count alone.

== Packaging and research constraints ==
TSVs and high-density package wiring connect memory layers and processing dies; yield, thermal gradients, repair strategies and signal integrity constrain implementation. Potential research directions include bank-aware scheduling, data placement, thermal-aware throttling and computation near memory. These are research questions, not claims of established performance gains. HBM is not a processor cache and does not eliminate data-movement or coherence costs.

